      'Abusive head trauma (AHT) is inflicted craniocerebral injury, classically from violent shaking with or without an impact, that carries some of the highest mortality and morbidity of any form of child physical abuse. The eye examiner\'s role is narrow but critical: a dilated, scleral-depressed examination of the retinal periphery — ideally photographed — can identify a hemorrhage pattern that is highly specific for AHT and materially changes how the receiving medical and child-protection teams proceed. The finding of suspicious retinal hemorrhage does not make the examiner responsible for proving abuse; it triggers an immediate, unconditional obligation to escalate to emergency medical care and to report to child protective services, in parallel with — not contingent on — building a fuller clinical picture.',
